QUESTION 2 : Why is Git called a Distributed Version Control System (DVCS)?
====================================================================

Interview Ready Answer

Git is called a Distributed Version Control System because every
developer has a complete copy of the repository, including the
entire commit history and branches, on their local machine.
Developers can work offline and synchronize their changes later
with a remote repository like GitHub.

Definition

Distributed = Complete repository on every developer's machine.

Version = Different snapshots/versions of the project.

Control = Managing and controlling different versions of source code.

Purpose

• Every developer has a full repository.
• Supports offline development.
• Maintains complete commit history locally.
• Enables efficient collaboration.

Memory Trick

Distributed = Repository is distributed, NOT the project.

====================================================================
INTERVIEW TRAP
====================================================================

Q:
Does "Distributed" mean the project is divided among developers?

Answer:
No.

It means every developer has a complete copy of the repository,
including its history and branches.

====================================================================
QUICK REVISION
====================================================================

• Distributed = Full repository on every machine.
• Version = Project snapshots.
• Control = Manage source code versions.
• Git works offline because the repository is local.